A failure body that is not JSON at all, so it is not a JSON:API error document either.

ASSEMBLED, not observed live. This is the shape a reverse proxy or load balancer in front of AB Connect
emits when the service behind it is unreachable, and it is the reason ParseErrors is specified to
return an empty list rather than to throw. The extension is .txt, not .json, because the content is
not JSON; every other fixture in this directory is genuinely the JSON it claims to be.

Relevant specification text, 01-sdk-v3.md section 5.2:

  When the body is not parseable as that shape the list is empty and the raw body's first 2000
  characters go into the exception message.

What a test must assert from it:
  - ABConnectResponseMapper.ParseErrors returns an EMPTY list. It must not throw, and it must not
    return null: ABConnectRequestException.Errors is documented as never null, [] when there are none.
  - The same holds for a body that parses as JSON but has no "errors" array, and for one whose
    "errors" member is not an array. Those two cases need no fixture file; they are one-line string
    literals in the test.
  - A 502 response carrying this body maps to ABConnectServerException with an empty Errors list, and
    the exception message carries an excerpt of the body so the operator can see it was an HTML gateway
    page rather than an AB Connect answer. Distinguishing those two is the whole value of the excerpt.
  - The excerpt is bounded by ABConnectResponseMapper.MaxBodyExcerptLength (2000). This body is far
    shorter than that, so a truncation test needs a synthesized long string rather than this file.
